---
title: Client tiers
summary: Why reading a model and changing it are separate traits, ModelReaderOps and ModelBuilderOps, drawn by what a call changes rather than by what it builds, so an estimator can be given one without the other.
order: 9
---

## Context

An estimator needs to evaluate and differentiate a model. It should not be able to change the model
it is estimating: add or delete terms, fix one of its unknowns by observing it, or re-root a
quantity.

`ModelClient` takes `&self` everywhere, is freely cloned, and holds its graph behind a lock, so
`&mut` guards nothing. A trait bound is the only compile-time way to hand out a model that cannot
be changed.

The tiers were first drawn around structure: a call went on the builder tier if it could break a
reference. That put `observe`, `demote_to_parameter`, `add_parameter` and a leaf `remove` on the
estimator's side, and every one of them changes the model. `demote_to_parameter` deletes a density
term outright.

## Decision

The tiers are drawn by what a call changes. The read tier may add nodes, but may not change:

- the log-density `p`,
- the proposal `q`,
- the set of free or observed quantities,
- or the batch sizes the model accepts.

Anything that changes one of those is on the builder tier.

```
{../../../../crates/skoro-model/src/client_ops.rs:276..297}
```

| Call | Tier | Why |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `evaluate*`, `log_density*`, `log_proposal`, `log_weight`, `generate*` | `ModelReaderOps` | Read the model. |
| `factors`, `terms`, `bound`, `bindings_of`, `tangent_layout` | `ModelReaderOps` | Inspect the model. |
| `add_constant` | `ModelReaderOps` | Adds a node, changes nothing else. |
| `cleanup` | `ModelReaderOps` | Takes only what nothing reaches. |
| `add_quantity`, `add_proposal`, `add_factor`, `bind` | `ModelBuilderOps` | Add terms. |
| `add_parameter` | `ModelBuilderOps` | Grows the free set. |
| `observe`, `unobserve` | `ModelBuilderOps` | Change the free and observed set. |
| `demote_to_parameter` | `ModelBuilderOps` | Deletes a term. |
| `add_batched_constant`, `stacked_tangent` | `ModelBuilderOps` | Pin the batch for the whole model. |
| `remove` | `ModelBuilderOps` | Leaf-only; never breaks a reference, but still removes a parameter or a term. |
| `remove_closure` | `ModelBuilderOps` | Takes the downward closure and its referrers. |

`Q` has no `observe`, for the same reason it has no `add_factor`. A `Q` carries a full client, so
anything on it is reachable by whoever holds the quantity, whatever bound the model came under.

There is no tier between the two. Marginalizing is `demote_to_parameter`, then a joint factor
over the blanket, then `remove_closure`. A tier that granted demotion without the rest would
permit the half-done state: a demoted quantity with nothing compensating for it. Marginalization
belongs on the builder tier as one operation.

## Consequences

- An estimator holding only `ModelReaderOps` cannot change the model it was handed, through the
  client or through a `Q`.
- Every negative claim is a compile-fail test in `crates/skoro-model/tests/compile_fail/`, one per
  call: `observe`, `demote_to_parameter`, `add_parameter`, `remove`, `remove_closure`, and
  `Q::observe`.
- The bound guards generic code only. Code holding a concrete `ModelClient` can do anything.
  There is no other way in: `server_call_mut` is private, and there is no route from a server's
  `Id` back to its client, so seeing a quantity is not a way to reach the model behind it.
- Neither trait says anything about what a model *contains*; see
  [Arity is the capability test](../no-capability-flags/).

> [!DECISION] "Can it break a reference?" was the wrong question
> Observing, demoting and adding a parameter cannot break a reference, and each one changes the
> model being estimated. The question is whether a call changes the model, not whether it damages
> the graph.
